===Etymology===
{{PIE word|en|tewtéh₂}}
1580, from {{der|en|la|Teutonicus}}, from {{m|la|Teutonēs}}, {{m|la|Teutonī|t=the Teutons|pos=name of a {{der|en|gem|-}} tribe that inhabited coastal Germany and devastated Gaul between 113–101 B.C.}}, equivalent to {{af|en|Teuton|-ic}}.<ref>[http://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0059:entry=teutoni Teutŏni], in Charlton T. Lewis and Charles Short (1879) A Latin Dictionary, Oxford: Clarendon Press</ref>.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˌtjuːˈtɒn.ɪk/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒnɪk|s=3}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Relating to the ancient [[Germanic]] people, the [[Teuton]]s.
# Having qualities that are regarded as typical of [[German]] people.
#: {{co|en|'''Teutonic''' exactitude}}
#* {{RQ:James Princess Casamassima|volume=3|page=190|passage=He waited and waited, in the faith that Schinkel was dealing with them in his slow, categorical '''Teutonic''' way, and only objurgated the cabinetmaker for having in the first place paltered with his sacred trust.{{...|Why hadn't he come straight to him—whatever the mysterious document was—instead of talking it over with French featherheads?}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Man Goes To See a Doctor|author=w:Adam Gopnik|date=1998-08-17|url=https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/1998/08/24/man-goes-to-see-a-doctor|work=The New Yorker|passage= My disorderliness was anathema to his '''Teutonic''' soul. “Here, I will write it down. Oh, you are so chaotic. Hand me the telephone.”}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} Relating to the [[Germanic]] branch of the [[Indo-European]] [[language family]].

